00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.230
- [Helen] Upon entering
the world of Stray,

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:51.690
we watch our darling,
lonesome kitten avatar

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.400
enter a post-apocalyptic city.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.610
Humanity, we learn, has been
killed off by a brutal pandemic

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.830
that only their robot companion survived.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.390
Too soon, too soon.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.640
The gameplay obviously
stands out from the get-go.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.460
You're constricted by the
ways an actual cat would move,

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.320
which obviously involves plenty of jumping

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.610
and knocking down boxes.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.370
The game's been lauded for its
realistic depiction of a cat,

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.830
a feat corroborated by
real life cat fandom.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.720
So by mimicking the abilities

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.030
and constraints of
inhabiting a cat's body,

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:19.980
are we actually learning
what it's like to be a cat?

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.690
According to philosopher Thomas Nagel

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.450
and his paper, "What is it
Like to be a Bat?", nah.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.070
Nagel argues that most living things

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.600
have a sense of consciousness.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.610
A "Subjective character of experience."

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.890
That means that there is
"Something that it is like

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.520
to be that organism, something
it is like for the organism."

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:36.750
But we can never really

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.880
know what that consciousness feels like.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.160
That's partially due to
our limited imaginations,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.470
as we inevitably map our
sense of human consciousness

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:44.340
onto other animals.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.980
But even if we animorphed
into a bat for a day,

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.990
our brains have been hardwired since birth

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.520
for human consciousness.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.950
Thus, we would still not
be experiencing things

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:56.790
as a bat, Nagel explained, but
as a person in a bat's body.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.120
Sure, we can simulate cathood
through adorable video games.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.920
But because we haven't
lived it from birth,

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.300
we can't fully understand it.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.000
But these limitations actually
open up interesting questions

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.380
about the robots we meet.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.780
Because the game positions us
from the cat's perspective,

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.610
it arguably seems to agree with Nagel,

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.800
that there is a consciousness
to be had in cathood,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.660
even if we can't quite comprehend it.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.610
Thus, we can also
presuppose the same thing

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:18.480
about the robots.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.310
They're not human as
much as they try to be,

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.230
but they have their own
kind of consciousness

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.020
and frame of reference, their
own robothood, if you will.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.960
But what if anything, do
robots share with humans?

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.910
Well, just as you can't truly experience

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.400
a cat's night vision, these
robots probably can't understand

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.200
the nuances of the human experience.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.180
Although they have,
apparently, taught themselves

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.080
to get crunk.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.100
But they're essentially living

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.570
in a Nagel thought experiment.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.160
As our robot pal explains,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.440
the companion started
out personality-free,

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.450
existing solely to perform tasks.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.640
But over time, they began mimicking humans

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:48.870
and forming societies.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.210
First, we encounter a
village called The Slums,

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.640
full of companions who are
portrayed as working class

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:54.930
or lower class folks,

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.630
and there's a sense of
melancholy, but not despair.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:58.950
It's an actual community,

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.330
one where they look out for each other.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:01.290
We learned that the robots

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.440
posthumously discovered humanity's
art, music, and society,

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.440
and this inspired them to try
to recreate the human world.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.020
Fittingly, then, The Slums
despite the grunginess,

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.205
are chock full of artistic vibes.

00:04:12.205 --> 00:04:15.612
(eclectic music)

00:04:15.612 --> 00:04:18.529
(robot chittering)

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.462
The robots seem to appreciate
all things aesthetic.

00:04:25.462 --> 00:04:28.710
(robot chittering)

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.990
They keep a beautiful
tree alive, just for fun,

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.270
and use neon lights, just
for the pretty colors.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.040
Musicians play music for free,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.810
as long as you deliver new song pages.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.120
Most of the interactions
you have here are bartering

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.050
or favor-based.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.370
If you collect someone's notebooks,

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.290
they'll do you a solid, and so on.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:46.080
This creates a sense of
a pre-industrial society,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.700
one where capitalism and commerce

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.200
haven't quite established a foothold.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.570
What we are seeing are
genuine signs of community,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.460
encapsulated in the way the elders sit

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.380
in silent, collective meditation.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:58.020
We learned that Momo, otherwise
considered an outsider,

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.240
is desperate to recover
his missing friends,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.650
which demonstrates how important
companionship is for him.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:05.490
(robot chittering)

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.710
When you rescue a long
lost scientist robot,

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:10.320
he's greeted with love by
his son and former friends.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.000
Everyone is basically kind to you.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.460
And even the grouchy Seamus,
we learn, is only rude

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.326
because he so missed his father.

00:05:16.326 --> 00:05:19.320
(robot chittering)

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.540
What we see is robots
interacting authentically

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:22.373
with one another,

00:05:22.373 --> 00:05:24.720
and it gives them a convincing
semblance of humanity.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.100
Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.750
famously talks about this concept

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.480
in the context of recognition,
which we've discussed before.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.490
Essentially, in order
to understand ourselves

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:33.750
as distinct individuals,

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:35.850
we need to be recognized
as such by others.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:37.350
Our personhood is thus established

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.270
through affirming interactions with others

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.280
who recognize us as human beings,

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.627
or as the Hegel-inspired
Jean-Paul Sartre put it,

00:05:43.627 --> 00:05:46.350
"The road of interiority
passes through the other."

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.550
Philosopher Charles Taylor

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.800
thinks recognition is
a "Vital human need."

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.930
And that "We need
relationships to fulfill,

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:53.400
but not define ourselves."

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.160
In this way, the robots
through recognizing one another

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.530
and affirming interactions
are providing one another

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.390
an essential aspect of humanness.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:02.100
Philosopher Axel Honneth contends

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.860
that recognition is essential
to self-actualization

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:05.790
and living freely.

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:06.810
This is only possible

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.450
if you are "An active member
of a social community,

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:10.920
the cohesion of which has emerged

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:13.770
from a mutually shared value
orientation toward freedom."

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.740
Basically, you need community
to be a fully formed person.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.560
Now, a more advanced
version of robot society

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:20.700
exists in Midtown,

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.070
which has a New York or
Hong Kong feel to it.

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.840
But we aren't in The Slums anymore.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.420
Here, arts and crafts
are replaced by commerce,

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.010
and just about every interaction

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:30.420
takes place in that context.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.910
And a sense of alienation
permeates this level.

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:34.200
In contrast to The Slums,

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.990
people in public places mostly
tend to keep to themselves.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.250
When you do try to engage,

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:40.765
many of the robots are
downright rude to you.

00:06:40.765 --> 00:06:42.600
(pensive music)

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.790
Public culture seems somewhat superficial.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:46.890
The closest thing to
community is the barbershop,

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:49.560
which is still, after
all, a commercial space.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.380
For the most part, these robots
seem defined by their labor,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.420
rather than by any semblance of community

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.160
or by extension, humanity.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:59.490
What we see here is what
philosophers call "misrecognition"

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:00.757
or as Honneth explains,

00:07:00.757 --> 00:07:03.000
"The withdrawal of social recognition,

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:05.820
in the phenomena of
humiliation and disrespect."

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:07.620
Robots are reduced to
their commercial value

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.120
and their ability to perform labor,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.590
rather than their unique identities

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:11.940
or attempts at humanness.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.820
Taylor argues that when
others misrecognize you,

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.800
it can make you misrecognize yourself.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.840
In the lonely nightclub, your companion,

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.180
who used to be a human,
points out what's missing.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.550
He used to drink with his
friends during the bad times,

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:26.548
and the joy of these interactions
gave their lives purpose.

00:07:26.548 --> 00:07:29.465
(robot chittering)

00:07:38.971 --> 00:07:40.230
He sees the lack of recognition

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.840
as essential to the apparent
unhappiness we witness

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:43.673
in Midtown.

00:07:43.673 --> 00:07:45.300
Indeed, commercial relations here

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:46.950
make for a cutthroat world.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.500
We explore an ugly factory
where workers are surveilled

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.260
by sentinel robots who also
act as police officers.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:55.024
One guy heartlessly gets
you arrested, saying-

00:07:55.024 --> 00:07:57.941
(robot chittering)

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:01.800
What we see here is that
society has progressed

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:04.290
from a pre-industrial
system of favors, bartering,

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:07.320
and small businesses, to a
cutthroat corporatized world.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.270
That shift is essential to the alienation

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:10.350
that permeates Midtown,

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:11.490
where everyone seems worried

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:12.690
that you're gonna steal from them.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:14.610
The transformation
recalls Rousseau's theory

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:16.170
about the dangers of private property.

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:17.880
He wrote, "The first man who,

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:19.590
having enclosed a piece of ground,

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:21.510
bethought himself of
saying 'this is mine,'

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.370
and found people simple
enough to believe him,

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.350
was the real founder of civil society."

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.120
Rousseau believed that
much human suffering

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:27.953
could have been avoided

00:08:27.953 --> 00:08:30.420
if everybody had told the
loser claiming the land

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:31.980
to (beep) off, because according to him,

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.140
civil society was a misguided step away

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:36.630
from the state of nature, which
is a morally neutral place

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:38.940
where people generally act
towards self-preservation

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:41.370
and with compassion, much
like what we saw in The Slums.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.410
It's fitting that in this
capitalist landscape,

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:45.990
a police state proliferates
via the sentinel robots.

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:47.400
This mirrors the real world.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.177
As historian Gary Potter explains,

00:08:49.177 --> 00:08:50.640
"The police are primarily there

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.030
to protect business property
first, not human interactions.

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.430
If that were the case,
they would fail miserably."

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.350
This suggests that a system of capitalism

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.960
would appear simultaneously
with a system of policing

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.120
to enforce that system
of private ownership.

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:05.610
And when we make it to the
final level, the Control Room,

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:08.160
we find an even more
alienating environment.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:11.010
Everything is antiseptic,
like a boring office building.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:11.940
The only nature

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.430
comes in the form of carefully
manicured centerpiece,

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:17.580
and the only art is a banal
postcard-like image of a beach.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:19.470
The robots are also completely different

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:21.390
from their downstairs counterparts.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.620
None of them have names

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.080
and they don't do anything
except work silently.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:26.520
When you try to ask them for help,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:27.630
they won't even look at you

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:29.970
and just offer vaguely
polite corporate speak.

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.100
Whereas other robots
personally engaged with you,

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:33.960
these robots arguably are participating

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.420
in what Taylor would
call "nonrecognition."

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.090
He writes that, "Nonrecognition
or misrecognition

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.520
can inflict harm, can
be a form of oppression,

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:42.420
imprisoning someone

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.150
in a false, distorted and
reduced mode of being."

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:46.590
We see that by living in a world

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:47.640
where they're not recognized

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.380
for anything other than
the work they perform,

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:52.350
the robots have been reduced
to the role of, well, robots.

00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:53.700
And that's in stark contrast

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.040
to their more human-like
counterparts on the lower levels.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.260
Interestingly enough,
many folks in The Slums

00:09:58.260 --> 00:09:59.790
dream of making it to the top,

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:01.140
where they assume life is better.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:02.460
However, your travels

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:04.110
from the peaceful community of The Slums

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.780
to the antiseptic depersonalization
of the Control Room

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.130
suggests they might be wrong.

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:11.370
The game seems to argue that
progress isn't always good,

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:14.130
and the gameplay slowly
reinforces this idea.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.470
You're able to converse
with just about any robot

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:18.090
in The Slums, but as you move up,

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.890
you have fewer options to engage.

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:22.800
In other words, you are
recognized more in The Slums,

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.390
just like the robots themselves.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:26.430
The importance of art
to the human experience

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:29.280
is also emphasized by the
game's focus on aesthetics

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:30.390
over expediency.

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:32.400
It's not about a race to finish the game.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.250
It's about enjoying the artistic
experience along the way.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:37.260
That's something the robots
in The Slums would know,

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:38.640
while the robots in the Control Room

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.400
seem incapable of thinking
beyond capitalist efficiency.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.620
So what can we learn about
our own world from this?

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.000
Well, the game seems to be saying

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:48.270
that a big part of what makes
the robots and us more human

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:50.250
is our ability to connect with one another

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:52.110
on an emotional and communal level.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:53.880
And as Ephemera Journal recalls,

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:55.290
in the early 20th century,

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:57.480
German philosopher
Walter Benjamin lamented

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.660
that long-term, meaningful
and deep human connections

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:03.390
have been supplanted by
short-term, isolated moments

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:05.580
that began to constitute human existence.

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:06.900
So what was the culprit?

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:08.730
You guessed it, capitalism.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:10.327
As the OG Karl Marx explained,

00:11:10.327 --> 00:11:13.470
"Alienation is the objective
structure of experience

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.480
and activity in capitalist society.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.760
Capitalist society
cannot exist without it.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:20.010
Capitalist society in its very essence

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:22.530
requires that people be
placed into such a structure

00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:25.350
and, even better, that they
come to believe and accept

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:26.970
that it is natural and just."

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:29.190
And that's exactly what we see in "Stray".

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:31.140
As the world becomes more capitalistic,

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.690
the robots become more
alienated from one another

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:37.350
and from you, which is wild
because you are so cute.

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:39.750
Their total absorption
into consumption and status

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:42.600
in Midtown goes hand in hand
with their lack of socializing.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:44.820
We're socializing only in
the context of commerce,

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:45.720
theft, and policing.

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:47.550
This alienation only escalates

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:49.710
as you make it to the top
floor, where you see robots

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:51.690
who have been completely
reduced to laborers,

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:54.480
clearly believing that their
tasks are natural and just.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.620
What all this suggests

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:58.800
is that progress under
capitalism has a major downside,

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:00.240
the breakdown of community.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:01.710
We see this even in Midtown,

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:04.110
where entry into practically
every community space

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:06.120
has a price, in contrast to The Slums

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:07.140
where you can roam freely.

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:09.720
What it seems to suggest is
that we become more robotic,

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:12.030
more alienated, and more
detached from one another

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:13.110
when we define ourselves

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:16.320
solely by our ability to perform
labor and pay for things.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:17.790
When we fail to foster communities

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:19.110
around mutual recognition,

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.570
we rob ourselves of something
fundamentally human.

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:23.580
Still, this is a largely optimistic game.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:24.510
Yes, it seems to say

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:26.310
that the efficient world
of the Control Room,

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:28.770
where robots indeed act
like robots, is bad.

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:30.630
And yes, the Control Room seems to mimic

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:32.670
aspects of our world
where we are, in fact,

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.350
reduced to our ability to perform labor.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.270
But what if there's a way out?

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:37.650
What if we can still recognize

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:39.810
the aspects of our fundamental humanity?

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.280
Your robot companion does as much

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:43.830
when he essentially destroys
himself to override the system

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:45.030
and save all the robots.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.190
This suggests that even as our own world

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:48.690
starts to feel like the Control Room,

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.250
that there's still hope for us.

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:52.740
Our basic humanity and
need for mutual recognition

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:55.020
can be our saving grace
and foster connection,

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:56.820
even in the worst of circumstances.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:12:59.190
And if all else fails,
you can go adopt a cat.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:00.180
But what do you think?

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.760
Is "Stray" a warning about
the perils of late capitalism?

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.770
Or are we reading way too much into a game

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.360
that your cat can probably beat you at?

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:07.380
Let us know in the comments.

